f:Tb6d,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is non-duality?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Non-duality (Advaita) is the philosophical position that ultimate reality is singular and undivided. All apparent distinctions — self/other, subject/object, individual/universe — are superimpositions on a single underlying consciousness."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is dualism in Indian philosophy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Dualism (Dvaita) holds that two categories of reality are eternally distinct — typically conscious souls (Purusha) and inert matter (Prakriti) in Samkhya, or God and individual souls in Madhva's Vedanta."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which is more popular — non-duality or dualism?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Both have enormous followings. Advaita Vedanta is the most internationally influential Indian philosophy. Dvaita has deep roots in South India and strongly influences the Bhakti movement. Both traditions have living practitioners in the millions."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you practice spirituality without taking a position?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Many practitioners of bhakti (devotion) remain effectively in a dualistic relationship with God their whole lives and reach liberation. The Bhagavad Gita itself says the paths of Jnana and Bhakti both lead to the same liberation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does Vishishtadvaita differ from both?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Vishishtadvaita ('qualified non-duality') accepts that reality is ultimately one (Brahman) but insists that souls and matter are real components within that oneness — like cells within a body. It occupies the middle ground."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is non-duality just another word for monism?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not exactly. Monism says 'there is only one substance.' Non-duality is more subtle — it says that the distinction between observer and observed, between 'this' and 'that,' is not ultimately real. Some non-dual traditions reject even the concept of 'one thing.'"}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does non-duality make ethics meaningless?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Non-dual teachers are consistent on this: at the relative (Vyavaharika) level, cause and effect, ethics, and relationships are fully real. Liberation doesn't dissolve ethics — it often deepens compassion by removing selfish motivation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What Western philosophy is closest to Advaita?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Spinoza's pantheism, Hegel's Absolute Idealism, and aspects of Neoplatonism are frequently compared. Schopenhauer explicitly studied Upanishadic thought. However, Indian non-duality includes specific meditative practices absent from Western counterparts."}}]}7:["$","$1","c",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"min-h-screen bg-background text-foreground selection:bg-orange-500/30 selection:text-orange-100 flex flex-col","children":[["$","$Le",null,{"slug":"non-duality-vs-dualism","pillar":"ancient-wisdom"}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$f"}}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"Article\",\"headline\":\"Non-Duality vs Dualism: Understanding the Philosophical Difference\",\"description\":\"Non-duality says reality is fundamentally One; dualism says it is Two. These two positions divide Indian philosophy and echo through every major spiritual tradition. A complete guide.\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/non-duality-vs-dualism\",\"mainEntityOfPage\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/non-duality-vs-dualism\",\"datePublished\":\"2026-03-04\",\"dateModified\":\"2026-03-04\",\"articleSection\":\"Ancient Wisdom\",\"keywords\":[\"non dual vs dual philosophy\"],\"about\":[{\"@type\":\"Thing\",\"name\":\"non dual vs dual philosophy\"}],\"isPartOf\":{\"@type\":\"WebPage\",\"name\":\"Ancient Wisdom\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/ancient-wisdom-philosophies\"},\"inLanguage\":\"en\",\"author\":{\"@type\":\"Organization\",\"name\":\"Sadhaka\"},\"publisher\":{\"@type\":\"Organization\",\"name\":\"Sadhaka\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com\"}}"}}],"$L10","$L11","$L12"]}],["$L13","$L14"],"$L15"]}]
